// Conformance row K25 — a spliced body's module-private `static` still resolves
// in a CONST position
//
// The hardest position for design 210's contract, and the one that shows why
// the contract is a list rather than a feeling. `SLOTS` is private to
// `embedmod`; `Buf.fill` names it as an array LENGTH and as a repeat COUNT.
// Both are compile-time-only, so `ArrayLiteral.repeat_count` is a declared
// ANNOTATION on purpose — the coroutine transform's hoist must never lift a
// constant into a runtime `let`. Every structural walker therefore steps over
// it, including the one that marks a spliced body's answers as carried, so the
// count came out of the splice as a bare name to be looked up in the entry
// module — where `SLOTS` is not a name.
//
// Design 84 hid this for std by merging std's statics into every compile and
// turning the accessibility gate off for a std-sourced body. A user module gets
// neither, so the position had to be covered rather than permitted: the marking
// walk is the one walk in the transform that visits annotation fields.
//
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --module-path embedmod={TESTDIR}/modules/embedmod
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 8

import embedmod.{Runner}

func main() {
    let b = Runner(tag: 1)
    print(b.fill())
}
